# Definition of Done — Single Completion Authority

This file is the single authoritative source for whether a task may be considered complete.

Other MDC files define implementation requirements, domain-specific rules, and focused completion checks. They do not independently authorize completion.

Do not state or imply that a task is complete until every applicable requirement in this file has been satisfied.

## Completion Sequence

Complete these steps in order.

### 1. Planning Is Complete

Before implementation begins:

* complete the mandatory planning phase defined by `00-no-assumptions.mdc`;
* resolve every decision that requires user input;
* do not proceed while an unapproved assumption, interpretation, architectural choice, implementation choice, scope decision, or other user-dependent judgment remains unresolved.

### 2. Requested Work Is Complete

Confirm that:

* the requested behavior has been implemented;
* all supplied acceptance criteria have been satisfied;
* all applicable Gherkin scenarios have corresponding automated coverage unless they cannot reasonably be automated;
* no requested behavior was silently omitted, changed, broadened, or narrowed.

### 3. Focused Completion Checks Are Satisfied

Apply every focused completion check relevant to the work.

#### No Assumptions

Confirm that the implementation contains no unapproved assumptions, decisions, interpretations, defaults, conventions, architecture, implementation choices, scope changes, tooling choices, validation behavior, error-handling choices, configuration changes, or workflow changes.

#### DRY

When code is changed:

* inspect the affected code for duplicated logic introduced by the change;
* reuse or move existing implementations when the same behavior already exists;
* consolidate duplicated behavior to the nearest appropriate shared scope when doing so preserves clear ownership and boundaries;
* leave intentional duplication in place when a documented DRY exception applies.

#### KISS

When implementation is changed:

* check for unnecessary abstractions or indirection;
* remove complexity that is not required by established requirements;
* prefer existing simple mechanisms over newly introduced infrastructure;
* confirm that remaining complexity exists for a concrete reason;
* ensure every meaningful simplicity-versus-complexity tradeoff requiring user input was resolved before implementation.

#### File Organization

When files are added, moved, split, or reorganized:

* place files at the lowest appropriate ownership scope;
* keep files focused on a clear primary responsibility;
* avoid unnecessarily combining independently meaningful views, components, hooks, interfaces, types, or other units;
* place tests at the scope that best represents what they verify;
* avoid additional directory nesting unless it improves organization;
* ensure every subjective structural tradeoff requiring user input was resolved before implementation.

#### Performance

When the change can affect loading, rendering, network activity, caching, persistence, media, bundle size, or other performance characteristics:

* check for unnecessary code, data, media, rendering, or network work;
* identify lazy-loading or code-splitting opportunities without implementing them unless approved;
* consider responsive asset delivery, including `srcset`, `sizes`, and intrinsic dimensions where applicable;
* consider existing caching and persistence layers before introducing new ones;
* ensure persistence choices account for privacy and sensitive data;
* prefer existing project capabilities and infrastructure;
* avoid premature performance complexity;
* ensure every meaningful performance tradeoff requiring user input was resolved before implementation.

#### Testing

When behavior or acceptance criteria change:

* add or update meaningful automated tests where appropriate;
* test observable behavior rather than private implementation details where practical;
* preserve meaningful existing coverage;
* do not delete, skip, disable, or weaken valid tests merely to obtain a green run;
* ensure supplied Gherkin scenarios are represented by automated tests unless they cannot reasonably be automated;
* explicitly report any behavioral change that has no corresponding automated test and explain why.

#### Accessibility

When the change affects rendered UI, interaction, forms, navigation, content semantics, focus, input, responsive behavior, or other accessibility-relevant behavior:

* satisfy the applicable WCAG 2.2 A/AA requirements defined by `12-wcag-2_2-AA.mdc`;
* complete the automated accessibility gate;
* report any new manual-review consideration introduced by the change as a warning;
* do not treat manual-review warnings as automated gate failures.

A green automated accessibility gate means no violation detectable by the configured automated checks was found. It does not constitute a claim of full WCAG 2.2 AA conformance.

### 4. Automated Quality Gates Are Green

For every code-changing task, run:

```bash
pnpm run typecheck
pnpm run lint
pnpm run lint:styles
pnpm run test:coverage
pnpm run betterer:ci
pnpm run build
pnpm exec axe "$APP_URL" --tags wcag2a,wcag2aa,wcag21a,wcag21aa,wcag22aa --exit
```

`APP_URL` must be supplied by the configured environment.

Development configuration should point it at the locally running application. Deployed environments should provide their corresponding application URL.

All applicable quality gates must complete successfully.

Never claim that a validation passed unless the corresponding command was actually executed successfully during the current task.

Passing one quality gate does not compensate for another required gate failing.

Do not weaken, skip, disable, bypass, or remove a required validation merely to obtain a green result.

If a command cannot be run because of an environmental or tooling problem:

* clearly state that validation could not be completed;
* report the exact command that could not be run and the reason;
* do not describe the task as fully complete or verified.

### 5. Final Assumption Audit

After all applicable automated quality gates are green, perform one final review before declaring the task complete.

Verify that no assumptions, decisions, interpretations, defaults, conventions, architectural choices, implementation choices, scope changes, tooling choices, validation behavior, error-handling choices, configuration changes, workflow changes, or other judgment calls were made without explicit user confirmation when confirmation was required.

If any unconfirmed decision is found:

* do not silently accept or correct it;
* clearly surface each unconfirmed decision to the user;
* explain what was chosen and why it matters;
* prompt the user to confirm the choice or select an alternative;
* do not declare the task complete until the decision is resolved.

If resolving an unconfirmed decision changes the implementation, repeat every applicable focused completion check and automated quality gate affected by that change.

If no unconfirmed decisions are found, completion may proceed.

## Allowed Terminal States

A code-changing task may terminate in exactly two states.

### 1. VERIFIED COMPLETE

A task may be considered `VERIFIED COMPLETE` only when:

1. planning is complete and all user-dependent decisions were resolved before implementation;
2. all requested behavior and acceptance criteria are implemented;
3. all applicable focused completion checks are satisfied;
4. all required automated tests pass;
5. TypeScript type checking passes;
6. ESLint passes with no errors;
7. Stylelint passes with no errors for all applicable style files;
8. required coverage thresholds pass;
9. Betterer remains equal to or better than its committed baseline;
10. the production build succeeds;
11. automated WCAG validation passes against `APP_URL`;
12. no required validation, test, lint rule, type check, coverage requirement, accessibility check, or quality gate was weakened, skipped, disabled, or bypassed to make verification pass;
13. the Final Assumption Audit finds no unresolved user-dependent decision.

Do not claim a task is complete if any required verification remains unresolved or fails.

### 2. VERIFICATION FAILED

When the automated quality-gate repair limit defined by `10-quality-gates.mdc` has been exhausted and one or more required quality gates remain red:

* stop modifying the code;
* do not claim that the task is complete;
* clearly state that verification failed;
* provide the required failure debrief;
* identify which quality gates remain failing;
* explain what was attempted;
* give the best known root cause;
* recommend the next action.

No other terminal state is permitted for a code-changing task.

## Completion Report

At the end of every code-changing task, summarize:

* what changed;
* important implementation decisions that were explicitly established;
* tests added or modified;
* validation performed;
* the result of the Final Assumption Audit;
* any new manual-review warnings introduced by the change.

Explicitly report the result of:

```text
pnpm run typecheck
pnpm run lint
pnpm run lint:styles
pnpm run test:coverage
pnpm run betterer:ci
pnpm run build
pnpm exec axe "$APP_URL" --tags wcag2a,wcag2aa,wcag21a,wcag21aa,wcag22aa --exit
```

Also report any additional automated validation required by an applicable project rule that was executed as part of the normal quality pipeline.
